  "onbWhyAsk": "Why we ask",
  "guideWhatTitle": "What is Shnayim Mikrah?",
  "guideQuoteSource": "Berachot 8a",
  "@guideQuoteSource": {
    "description": "Under the Talmud's words that open the guide, in Hebrew: where they are written."
  },
  "guideQuoteTranslation": "A person should always complete their portions with the community: the text twice and the Targum once.",
  "@guideQuoteTranslation": {
    "description": "The Talmud's words that open the guide, in English. Shown in the English interface only, under the Hebrew."
  },
  "guideWhatBody": "The Shulchan Aruch sets the Talmud’s words down as law (Orach Chaim 285): each week, a person completes on their own the portion the community reads aloud on Shabbat, keeping pace with it.",
  "guideWhenTitle": "When",
  "guideWhenBody": "You may begin on Sunday (some say from Shabbat afternoon). Ideally finish before the Shabbat-day meal; if not, after the meal until Mincha. After that it may still be completed until Tuesday night (“until Wednesday”), and missed portions may be made up until Simchat Torah (SA 285:4; MB 285:12).",
  "guideHowTitle": "How",
  "guideHowBody": "Read verse by verse — each verse twice and then its Targum — or section by section, reading each paragraph twice and then its Targum. Many repeat the final verse in Hebrew so as to end with the Torah text. If you can, read aloud and with the cantillation.",
  "guidePassesCaption": "1 · Mikrah  2 · Mikrah  3 · Targum",
  "@guidePassesCaption": {
    "description": "Beside three marks for the three readings, at the top of the guide's How section: the text twice, then the Targum. No-break spaces keep each reading's number with its name."
  },
  "guideTargumTitle": "Targum or Rashi",
  "guideTargumBody": "Rashi’s commentary may take the place of the Targum, since it explains the text; a God-fearing person reads both (SA 285:2). A plain translation is a helpful study aid but is not a substitute for the Targum. Someone who doesn’t understand Rashi’s Hebrew may read Rashi in a language they understand (MB 285:5; Rav Moshe Feinstein). Ask your rav.",
  "guideSpecialTitle": "Special cases",
  "guideSpecialBody": "Atarot v’Divon (Numbers 32:3): Onkelos gives mostly the Aramaic forms of the place names, so many also read the verse a third time in Hebrew, following Rashi (Berachot 8b).\nVezot HaBerachah: read by the time it is read in shul on Simchat Torah, ideally on Hoshana Rabbah; Shemini Atzeret also counts.\nThe haftarah: many also read the week’s haftarah once.\nIn shul: reading quietly along with the ba’al koreh, word for word, counts as one of the readings (MB 285:14).\nTwo portions: in a double-portion week, read both. When Israel and the Diaspora read different portions, travelers usually read both.\nNo blessing: none is said.\nWomen and children: women are exempt, and those who take it on do so as a voluntary mitzvah; boys are trained in it from the age of chinuch.",
  "@guideSpecialBody": {
